  "query_preview": "..A Pacific Northwest low is centered over northwest\nArizona this evening. A moist and unstable northerly flow remains\nin place over Utah and southwest Wyoming under the influence of\nthis system, though associated valley rain and mountain snow has\nbecome increasingly spotty. Along with the precipitation, the\nsystem has also brought quite unseasonably cold temperatures, with\nmaxes today having run 15-20F below seasonal normals. With some\npockets of clearing in this airmass, some outlying valleys over\nwestern Utah will see lows near or below freezing, which could\nbring some impacts to agricultural activity. Going Freeze Warning\ncovers this threat well. The threat of freezing lows will continue\nthrough at least Thursday morning.\n\nThe low is on track to trek across Arizona during the morning\nbefore sliding northeast into Colorado Wednesday afternoon,\nnoticeably slower than what guidance was indicating yesterday,\nanother example of models struggling with closed lows. Thus, the\nthreat of showers will continue tomorrow, focused primarily over\neastern Utah. The position of the low could also bring some\nlocally gusty winds. Currently, the north/northeasterly flow is\ncreating a pressure gradient modestly favorable for gap winds over\nprone areas of Washington county. However, associated cold\nadvection is unimpressive, limiting the threat. By Wednesday\nnight/Thursday morning, the position of the low will bring a more\nuncommon east to northeast flow over much of the area, which would\nbring some canyon winds to prone areas of the Wasatch Front. As is\nthe case with the Washington county wind threat, the associated\ncold advection over northern Utah and southwest Wyoming is modest\nat best, keeping the chance of downslope winds low. \n\nBeginning Thursday, high pressure will start to build off the\nCalifornia coast, interacting with another low near Baja\nCalifornia to produce a weak Rex Block pattern, bringing drier air\nand a slow warming trend. The block is expected to be dislodged by\nSaturday as a trough moves across the US/Canada border. The\nimpacts will be minimal for Utah and southwest Wyoming, however,\nwith dry conditions continuing with temperatures moving back to\nnear seasonal normals. \n\nThe loss of the Pacific ridge again opens up the storm track for\nthe potential of a more active pattern across the West beginning\nearly next week. Despite this, there remains noticeable spread as\nto when a storm system might impact the area, with chances\nincreasing beginning Tuesday. Given the level of uncertainty,\ndetails on any particular system remain dim at this time.",
